# port-patches.sh: keep-p0 @ 820d104ac9ac over glm/zig-engine-fixes-on-186 @ e027c49cd06d (commits cf4dde927470..820d104ac9ac); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
glm_cuda: pool and kept-state observation (P0 of the pool fix): sizing, occupancy, copy timings, spill rates, request trace

Phase P0 of the plan to keep prompt states in the KV pool (Mia #185). It adds
measurements and log lines and changes no behaviour. Nothing is added to the
agreed settings text, no note or collective is sent, no allocation, placement,
keep or admission decision changes. Lines that need the prompt cache, the lane
host or the tier's leader come from rank 0; what runs on both ranks reads local
state and sends nothing.

What it logs
- Pool sizing, every rank: "KV pool sizing" gives the free memory the room was
  read from, the reserve, the room, the KV_POOL_GIB cap, what the pool was
  allowed, and the rows and bytes it took. cacheFor then logs "post-pool
  drift": MemAvailable now against MemAvailable at sizing, less the pool's own
  bytes, which is the figure the carve's pad has to cover (plan 1.6).
- Start line, rank 0, also with a 0 budget: "keep in pool: off; pool ..." with
  the pool and part rows, the largest keepable state, the estimated largest
  warm resume by a local copy and by a carry, the fixed MiB and bytes a token
  of a state, the entry cap and what binds it, the byte and per-chat caps, the
  fixed-state budget for the entries (in integers, rounded up, as the carve
  will take it), and the display cut. A DRM span gives its cut; dispram's one
  allocation has none and is logged as reserved, not as none. Each rank that
  reserves display memory also logs its own "display cut" line.
- "pool occupancy" every TF_GLM_POOL_LOG_S seconds on rank 0 (60; 0: none): for
  each part the live, twin, kept (0: no kept state lives in the pool) and free
  rows, the longest free run, and the published pool_free. The lane layer
  ticks it between decode rounds, fill steps and whole passes, beside the
  layout digest, so a server doing nothing logs nothing; it reads the
  host-side pool and sends nothing.
- Kept-state copies: takeSnap, restoreSnap, a carry and a state buffer's free
  are timed in phases (cuMemAlloc, copy calls, stream wait; a free's cuMemFree)
  with their bytes. Copies of TF_GLM_SNAP_LOG_MIB or more (64; 0: none, and no
  start-up probe) are logged by rank 0, and every copy is counted on atomics in
  /health's "snap_timing": for each operation a row for each size class
  (64 MiB, then each doubling) with the count, the bytes, the phases as totals
  over the row's copies (alloc_ms_total, copy_ms_total, sync_ms_total; a
  restore has the last two, a carry only enqueue_ms_total, a free only
  free_ms_total) and max_ms, the slowest copy. A carry is timed to the enqueue
  of its calls; its transfer lands in the restore's sync. A restore is timed
  after its carry returns (the carry has its own line and row), and its rate is
  over the bytes this rank copied (all but the latent rows it sent or had
  carried). A start-up probe logs the min, median and max cuMemAlloc and
  cuMemFree of a buffer the size of a fixed state.
- Spill tier, from its own counters: a line for each point written (queued to
  published, or why not), a line for each restore (bytes read, begin to the
  .validate verdict, the gather itself), and every TF_GLM_POOL_LOG_S seconds a
  rate line, rank 0's: bytes staged a second while a stream decodes (a lane
  still filling its prompt is not one) and while idle, restores, drains, and
  the disk written (GiB an hour, this rank and the other).
- "request trace v1", rank 0, one line when a request ends: its arrival, the
  prompt, cached and max_tokens counts, a hash of its conversation id, the time
  to its first token and to its end, the states its own pass kept (new states
  only, counted per request, so fills that overlap do not count each other's; a
  warm pass counts the state at its end), the conversation's states after the
  pass, the reply tokens and how it ended. Lengths only. TF_GLM_TRACE=0 turns it
  off; a header line gives the run's salt and the store (store=on
  budget=<bytes>, or store=off: a host with no prompt cache keeps and resumes
  nothing, so its lines read chat=0 cached=0 kept=0 entries=0). The Header,
  header, parseHeader, parse and parseAll of lanes.request_trace are what the
  P1 replay simulator reads.

Switches, all observation-only and local to each rank: TF_GLM_POOL_LOG_S (60),
TF_GLM_SNAP_LOG_MIB (64; 0 also skips the start-up probe), TF_GLM_TRACE (on). A
value that does not parse keeps its default and warns. With the defaults the
engine allocates, places, keeps and replies as before.

Tests: the formats of every line, the sizing and drift arithmetic, the
occupancy read of a plain and a sharded pool, its widest line in its buffer and
its rank gate, the histogram and its /health rows (each phase under its own
name), the spill rate windows (and the tier rig's write, restore and window),
the trace and its header round trip, its parse out of a log of all the other
lines, the lane layer's ticks (a decode round, a fill step, a whole pass) and
which lanes are decoding, and the lane host's trace of a conversation's turns,
two prompts filling at once (sliced and stepped), a warm pass, a request
cancelled before its pass, and a host with the trace off or on at the default
sink. test-glm-cuda-host: 404 passed (376 before), 2 skipped, the same 9
loopback failures as r8b. engine_api: 214 passed. Server tests: 203 passed, the
same recipe-flags failure as r8b (the /metrics 0045 test hangs on macOS
loopback and was skipped locally, not in this commit). The aarch64 cross build
is clean. Not GPU-validated.

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo).
Apply from the checkout root with: git apply 0039-zig-glm-pool-occupancy.patch

